course: dictionary
lesson: 14836
title: Radiocarbon: reading the clock in old things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Radiocarbon is a kind of carbon called carbon-14. It is slightly radioactive, which means it slowly breaks down over thousands of years. Scientists use this slow change as a clock. «Scientists used radiocarbon dating to find out the wooden bowl was 3,000 years old.» Dating here means finding out how old something is.
explain: All living things take in carbon-14 while they are alive, from air and food. After they die, the carbon-14 slowly disappears. So scientists measure how much is left in a wood, bone or piece of cloth. The less that is left, the longer ago it died. «The bone's radiocarbon age was about 10,000 years.» Radiocarbon age means the age found by this method.
explain: Use context to check your meaning. Read the words around radiocarbon: dating, age, and test all tell you it is about finding out how old something is. Try swapping in your meaning: "the radiocarbon test showed the cloth was made long ago" still makes sense when you think of a test for age.
fact: Radiocarbon means carbon-14, a kind of carbon that is slightly radioactive.
fact: Radiocarbon is slowly broken down over thousands of years, so it works like a clock.
fact: All living things take in carbon-14 while they are alive.
fact: «Radiocarbon dating» finds the age of once-living things, such as wood, bone and cloth, up to about 50,000 years.
fact: Radiocarbon is a noun when it means carbon-14, and an adjective when it describes a method, as in «radiocarbon test».
exercise: «Scientists used radiocarbon dating to find out the wooden bowl was 3,000 years old.» What does radiocarbon dating mean here? | Painting a bowl with bright colours | *Finding out how old something is using carbon-14 | Cutting wood into thin pieces | Measuring how heavy an object is
exercise: Radiocarbon is a kind of what? | Sand | *Carbon | Plastic | Metal
exercise: «The radiocarbon test showed the cloth was made long ago.» What is a radiocarbon test used for? | Making cloth stronger | Painting a picture | Cooking food | *Telling how old something is
test: Why do scientists measure the carbon-14 left in a bone? | To make the bone heavier | To see how much the bone weighs | *To find out how long ago the living thing died | To change the colour of the bone
test: Radiocarbon dating can tell the age of once-living things up to about how many years? | *About 50,000 years | 5 million years | 1,000 years | 500 years
exam: «The museum said the basket's radiocarbon age was 2,000 years.» What does radiocarbon age mean? | The price of the basket | *The age found by measuring the carbon-14 left in it | The colour of the basket | The weight of the basket
